00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.660
Airlines are pragmatic.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:22.640
Each, inherently, is a sizable organization
as a degree of scale is necessary due to the

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.960
massive capital costs needed to, quite literally,
get a plane off the ground.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.080
There are no mom and pop airlines.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.980
Therefore, airlines have the resources to
analyze choices to a far greater level of

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:41.260
detail than most businesses—any decision
that we, the public, see has passed through

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:48.090
such a level of review, such a level of scrutiny,
that it must mean something.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.300
Airlines typically don’t make emotional,
informal, or off-the-cuff decisions.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.090
Therefore, it’s odd how much airlines care
about climate change because, from a theoretical

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.200
perspective, they shouldn’t.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.680
It doesn’t currently make sense for airlines
to care about climate change.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.710
Of course, from a moral perspective it does,
but businesses, traditionally and theoretically,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.659
don’t value morality higher than profit.

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:21.090
That’s the role of non-profits—the organizational
structure designed to value social impact

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.400
first and foremost.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.460
For-profit business, in the traditional sense,
are designed for profit.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:33.689
Therefore, properly managed, they do what
generates the most profit, and for airlines,

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:39.170
right now, what enables that is to not care
about climate change.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.120
Carbon doesn’t have a cost for airlines.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.780
With a few exceptions, emitting more carbon
does not cost the airlines anything, but it

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.010
enables them to make more profit, so the theoretically
correct thing for airlines to do is to emit

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:57.180
the amount of carbon that enables them to
generate the most profit, regardless of environmental

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.420
consequences.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.790
Some say airlines care about climate change
for public relations, and they’re right—but

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.549
only to an extent.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:08.509
Airlines clearly posture as caring about their
carbon footprint because consumers increasingly

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.930
care about their carbon footprint, so being
viewed as a greener airline translates to

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:18.420
being a more desirable choice for consumers—who,
unlike for-profit businesses, do care about

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.840
what’s morally right to an extent.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.610
However, net carbon-zero is expensive, biofuel
development is expensive, sustainable aircraft

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:31.360
development is expensive—all more expensive
than would make sense if airlines and aircraft

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.060
developers exclusively cared about PR.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.200
So, the reason why airlines are starting to
care about climate change is because they’re

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.110
terrified of the r-word: regulation.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.330
It’s started already.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:49.660
In France, the national assembly has passed
a bill that would ban short-haul flights on

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.980
routes that could be travelled in two and
a half hours or less by train.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:03:00.209
Here’s the worst thing for airlines: measures
like this make absolute, perfect sense.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:04.900
With airports located away from city centers,
security lines, less reliable departure times,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.850
and more, no journey that would take two and
half hours by train could reasonably and reliably

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.080
be accomplished faster by plane.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.569
The bill would still allow these shorter flights
to operate for the purposes of connections

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:20.240
to longer-haul flights, but passengers just
couldn’t book the short flights by themselves.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.250
So, there’s no real downside, but there’s
the massive upside of shifting travelers onto

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:30.099
trains, which in the case of France emit 77
times less carbon per passenger on journeys

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:31.370
of this length.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.260
While this bill faces an uphill battle as
it needs to pass through the more conservative

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.610
French senate before enactment, it’s no
wonder why similar measures have been seriously

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.540
considered or implemented in the Belgian region
of Wallonia, Austria, the Netherlands, and

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.750
by countless employers globally.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.959
The problem for airlines is that they just
don’t have any good arguments against regulations

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:53.130
like these.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:59.180
A move away from the shortest-haul, most replaceable
flights will be the start, but it won’t

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.180
end there.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.989
As one of the more carbon-intensive industries,
airlines will face increasing regulatory barriers

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:13.190
to their operations—unless, of course, flying
becomes less carbon-intensive.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.930
Flying must happen, in many cases.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.289
Governments will not ban flying, full-stop.

00:04:20.289 --> 00:04:22.159
Governments will not heavily restrict flying,
full-stop.

00:04:22.159 --> 00:04:27.900
But governments will make it so the externality,
the carbon footprint, is priced in to the

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.310
cost of flying.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.110
That is, governments will create structures
in which greener flying is cheaper flying

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.059
with, for example, hefty carbon taxes.

00:04:38.059 --> 00:04:44.009
So, why airlines care about climate change
is because airlines are pragmatic, and its

00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:49.589
quickly and increasingly become the case that
competitiveness, as companies, is linked to

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:52.650
how green they are.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:58.219
Biofuels, engine efficiency improvements,
carbon offsetting—these things all help,

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:03.080
but they’re incremental changes that can
only take flying from being incredibly carbon-intense

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.060
to very carbon-intense.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:12.309
However, there is one solution that can actually,
realistically turn flying into a somewhat

00:05:12.309 --> 00:05:16.979
sustainable method of transport: electric
aircraft.

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:23.319
Now, here’s the thing: electric aircraft
are no massive innovation.

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:29.719
They existed in 1973 when the MB-E1 became
the first to fly with a human crew, they existed

00:05:29.719 --> 00:05:34.840
in 2015 when the Solar Impulse 2 circumnavigated
the world exclusively on solar power, and

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.449
they existed in 2020 when the Pipistrel Velis
Electro became the first commercially-available

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:41.819
type-rated electric-powered aircraft.

00:05:41.819 --> 00:05:47.089
There’s not a whole lot technologically
that has or is preventing electric aircraft

00:05:47.089 --> 00:05:48.089
from existing.

00:05:48.089 --> 00:05:51.919
What is is the business case.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:56.619
Batteries are just more expensive and less
energy dense than fuel, so with carbon emissions

00:05:56.619 --> 00:06:02.800
always having been free for airlines, it just
always made sense to use petroleum-based fuel.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.939
Aircraft development is an unbelievably expensive
endeavor, and getting an aircraft certified

00:06:06.939 --> 00:06:11.740
for commercial use using a new type of motor
is just all that much more, so it never made

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.789
sense for a manufacturer to go through all
that for an aircraft that didn’t have a

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:17.279
strong business case.

00:06:17.279 --> 00:06:24.139
Now, however, for the first time ever, a strong
hypothesis of a business case has developed

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:29.749
and, if it works, we will see the the most
dramatic shift in how commercial aircraft

00:06:29.749 --> 00:06:32.939
work in generations.

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:36.930
Electric aircraft inherently have constraints.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.939
While much progress has been made in the past
decade to bring down the price of batteries,

00:06:40.939 --> 00:06:45.770
and they now cost 1/10th as much per kilowatt
hour compared to a decade ago, less has been

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:51.389
done to increase battery energy density—that
is, the amount of electricity stored per pound

00:06:51.389 --> 00:06:52.499
or kilogram.

00:06:52.499 --> 00:06:57.910
That’s because, with electric vehicles,
the primary novel application, weight doesn’t

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:02.809
matter that much—it’s easier to increase
range by bringing down cost and putting more

00:07:02.809 --> 00:07:05.539
batteries in than improving their energy density.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:08.319
It’s different with aircraft.

00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:12.419
The lighter they are, the further they can
fly, so there are diminishing returns in adding

00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:16.709
additional batteries since doing so means
one needs to add even more batteries to provide

00:07:16.709 --> 00:07:21.139
the energy to carry the weight of those batteries
and so on and so forth.

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:26.159
Also, for now, all viable electric aircraft
are propeller-driven, specifically because

00:07:26.159 --> 00:07:30.389
it would be far more costly to develop an
electric jet engine, and the time savings

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:34.029
it would offer on the short-range flights
that electric aircraft would be capable of

00:07:34.029 --> 00:07:36.229
completing would be negligible.

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:43.099
So, airlines need to find a use case for short-range,
slow, electric aircraft.

00:07:43.099 --> 00:07:47.179
Conveniently, it already exists.

00:07:47.179 --> 00:07:52.899
From their Denver, Colorado hub, United Airlines
flies to sixteen destinations less than 250

00:07:52.899 --> 00:07:55.460
miles or 400 kilometers away.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:59.939
In particular in this case, many of these
destinations, such as Montrose, Aspen, Eagle

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:05.179
County, and Hayden, are high-yield and high-frequency
despite their close distance since they connect

00:08:05.179 --> 00:08:07.039
tourists to popular ski towns.

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:13.249
In the case of Widerøe, a Norwegian regional
airline, 19 of their 47 overall destinations

00:08:13.249 --> 00:08:17.909
are within 250 miles of their Tromso hub,
and a strong majority of their routes are

00:08:17.909 --> 00:08:20.959
over distances less than this.

00:08:20.959 --> 00:08:27.050
Then, there are airlines like Cape Air, who
do not fly a single route over 250 miles in

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.050
length.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:32.491
The use case for electric aircraft already
exists, because airlines like Cape Air have

00:08:32.491 --> 00:08:36.659
already found it, just not yet using electric
aircraft.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:42.130
They fly from large hubs to small destinations
on 9-seat propeller aircraft, and partner

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.420
with mainline airlines to offer connecting
itineraries.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.509
For them, the electric aircraft range constraints
don’t matter since all their routes are

00:08:49.509 --> 00:08:53.540
so short, and therefore the speed constraints
don’t either.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.260
When Cape Air flies from Martha’s Vineyard
to New York JFK on their propeller-driven

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:02.290
Cessna 402, the flight takes an average of
70 minutes, whereas Delta, flying the same

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:06.170
route using a jet CRJ200, takes 45.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:11.279
When Cape Air flies from Nantucket to Boston
Logan airport, they take 39 minutes, whereas

00:09:11.279 --> 00:09:15.270
JetBlue, on their E190 aircraft, takes 28.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:20.690
Those extra 11 or 25 minutes are negligible
in the overall travel experience, especially

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:25.019
considering that Cape Air’s boarding times,
with 9 passengers, are certainly quicker than

00:09:25.019 --> 00:09:28.310
with Delta’s 50 passengers or JetBlue’s
100.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:33.149
So, not only could electric aircraft seamlessly
replace all of Cape Air’s flights, but they

00:09:33.149 --> 00:09:38.050
could also take over some of Delta, JetBlue,
United, or other larger airlines’ routes

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:41.399
without a negative impact to overall trip
time.

00:09:41.399 --> 00:09:46.029
So, the use case exists, passengers would
be accept the switch, but what do electric

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:50.512
aircraft do for airlines, aside from enabling
a continuation of operations in the face of

00:09:50.512 --> 00:09:51.910
increasing regulation?

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:57.819
A very specific answer for this exists thanks
to one short document.

00:09:57.819 --> 00:10:02.629
This is the US Department of Transportation’s
Order Re-Selecting Airline document for their

00:10:02.629 --> 00:10:07.620
essential air service subsidized flights from
Boston Logan Airport to Augusta and Rockland,

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:08.620
Maine.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:13.529
You see, the US government subsidizes routes
like these to small, remote communities across

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:18.329
the country, in order to improve connectivity
and economic opportunity for these areas.

00:10:18.329 --> 00:10:23.600
As it’s the US taxpayer funding these programs,
the bids from airlines to operate these routes

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.899
are a matter of public record, providing a
truly unique glimpse into accurate operating

00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:30.509
costs for a single airline route.

00:10:30.509 --> 00:10:35.221
While they competed against Boutique Air,
Cape Air ended up winning the bid, so they

00:10:35.221 --> 00:10:37.760
now operate the route to Rockland, Maine.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:46.480
In exchange, they received a subsidy of $2,218,126
for their first year, 2018 to 2019, ramping

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.600
up to $2,495,090 by their fourth.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:55.310
Rockland is a tourist hotspot in the summer,
but it returns to a quieter state for the

00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:00.449
rest of the year, so the airline’s obligations
for flight frequency varies by the season—anywhere

00:11:00.449 --> 00:11:04.500
between three daily round-trip flights in
winter, to six in the summer.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:10.310
All in all, though, they’re obligated to
operate some 1,365 flights annually, representing

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:15.610
12,285 total available passenger seats in
each direction.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:22.209
According to Cape Air’s estimates, this
route should cost some $3,350,746 a year to

00:11:22.209 --> 00:11:23.209
operate.

00:11:23.209 --> 00:11:28.910
They estimated some 15,122 total passengers
would fly, but publicly available data shows

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:34.339
that only 6,733 boarded their planes in Rockland
in 2019.

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:37.890
Assuming it’s roughly the same in the other
direction, as one would expect, that means

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:45.550
they saw only 13,466 total passengers, representing
a 54.8% load factor.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:49.779
Selling tickets for an average of $83, the
airline likely therefore brought in $1,117,678

00:11:49.779 --> 00:11:59.240
in passenger revenue which, combined with
their $2,247,721 year two subsidy, means they

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:04.160
earned some $3,365,399 in revenue.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:11.970
Subtracting their annual cost estimates, that
means they only turned $14,653 in profit or

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:15.829
$1.09 worth per passenger.

00:12:15.829 --> 00:12:20.709
This is how tight airline profit margins can
be.

00:12:20.709 --> 00:12:24.670
But what would they be if this route was operated
by an electric airplane?

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:30.490
Well, to start, fuel costs would be almost
entirely eliminated.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.529
Electricity isn’t free, but its costs are
so low compared to fuel that it essentially

00:12:34.529 --> 00:12:36.990
becomes a non-factor.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.339
Electricity can also be converted into a capital
cost, instead of ongoing cost, through investing

00:12:41.339 --> 00:12:46.249
in solar or wind energy generation, which
Cape Air has hinted at doing.

00:12:46.249 --> 00:12:54.460
So, this $844,002 in annual fuel costs—we
can consider it, for all intents and purposes,

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:55.460
eliminated.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.720
Other costs would go up.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:05.180
Cape Air budgets $269,903 per year on ownership
costs—meaning, the amortized, prorated cost

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.209
of owning their aircraft.

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:11.610
While details are not known, and so an accurate
estimate would be impossible, there’s no

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:16.709
world in which a new, electric aircraft is
cheaper than the mass-produced, long-proven

00:13:16.709 --> 00:13:18.579
Cessna 402.

00:13:18.579 --> 00:13:23.310
It would’t be unreasonable to expect that
ownership costs would, say, double.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.959
Then, there are the maintenance costs.

00:13:25.959 --> 00:13:30.840
These would be significantly lower than with
traditional internal combustion driven aircraft.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.610
The thing is, electric motors just so simple.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:38.740
They have exactly one moving part—the shaft.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:42.509
Electric vehicles typically never require
motor maintenance, as there’s really just

00:13:42.509 --> 00:13:45.639
nothing that can break or wear down within
their lifetimes.

00:13:45.639 --> 00:13:50.259
This same principle will apply to electric
airplanes, as so much of the maintenance work

00:13:50.259 --> 00:13:53.690
aircraft require currently is on their engines.

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:57.850
While its very tough to know with any confidence
how this will play out in reality with planes

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.579
that don’t yet exist, there’s a world
in which aircraft maintenance would cost a

00:14:01.579 --> 00:14:04.040
fraction as much.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:09.450
Overall, these three factors combined—a
near-total elimination of fuel costs, a dramatic

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:14.389
increase in ownership costs, and a dramatic
decrease in maintenance costs—track with

00:14:14.389 --> 00:14:19.629
Cape Air’s estimates for a 40% reduction
in operating costs.

00:14:19.629 --> 00:14:22.680
That is enormous.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:29.079
On their Rockland route, assuming no change
in revenue, that would take $1.09 in per passenger

00:14:29.079 --> 00:14:37.110
profit, and turn it into $100.62—a number
so dramatically different that it doesn’t

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.089
even sound correct.

00:14:39.089 --> 00:14:44.630
This would immediately turn Cape Air into
the highest operating-margin airline in the

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.699
world—full-stop.

00:14:46.699 --> 00:14:52.470
This is why the future of short-haul flying
is inevitably electric.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:59.809
This is why, in 2019, Cape Air became the
first commercial customer to order the Alice—a

00:14:59.809 --> 00:15:05.059
nine-passenger electric aircraft developed
by start-up aircraft manufacturer Eviation.

00:15:05.059 --> 00:15:10.800
It will be able to fly each and every one
of the airline’s routes roundtrip without

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:11.959
charging.

00:15:11.959 --> 00:15:15.970
There are certainly no guarantees that the
Alice will see the light of day—aircraft

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:20.279
development is an incredibly costly, risky,
and long process that can spell failure for

00:15:20.279 --> 00:15:25.829
even the strongest concepts—but everything
so far suggests that Eviation has just as

00:15:25.829 --> 00:15:30.949
much a shot at success as all the other players
that have entered this race.

00:15:30.949 --> 00:15:34.980
While Cape Air may be pioneering the use of
these aircraft in the commercial context,

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.920
as they have the advantage of a preexisting
route network and business strategy that are

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:44.149
conducive to their use, the release of such
aircraft will fundamentally break a long-held

00:15:44.149 --> 00:15:46.619
truth about aviation.

00:15:46.619 --> 00:15:51.170
Short-haul flying is currently more expensive,
per mile, than long-haul flying, due to the

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:55.930
high fixed costs involved with the ground
operations at each airport.

00:15:55.930 --> 00:16:00.270
However, with electric aircraft available
exclusively on shorter routes, at least in

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:05.490
the near-future, there’s a reality in which
its cheaper per mile to fly 200 miles than

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:06.589
2,000 miles.

00:16:06.589 --> 00:16:11.920
That will shift airlines’ focus to more
regional route networks, and connect communities

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:15.899
that previously were too small to make economic
sense for airlines.

00:16:15.899 --> 00:16:21.449
It short, not only will electric aircraft
allow airlines to continue to operate short-haul

00:16:21.449 --> 00:16:27.200
as regulations ramp up, but it will allow
them to increase it in a sustainable, profitable

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.629
way.

00:16:28.629 --> 00:16:33.689
The future of flying is inevitably electric,
because the business case is just too strong

00:16:33.689 --> 00:16:37.240
to ignore.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:41.779
Plenty of progress has already been made to
make electric flying a reality, and as much

00:16:41.779 --> 00:16:45.009
as I’d like to tell you in detail about
the specific electric aircraft that you might

00:16:45.009 --> 00:16:50.670
fly in a matter of years and how they work,
there just isn’t the time in this video.

